<h2>Chapter 3: Local SEO for Contractors — Dominate the Map Pack Without a Storefront</h2>
<p>Local SEO is the highest-ROI marketing channel for general contractors who serve a defined geographic area. The data is unambiguous: 46% of all Google searches are local (Changescape Web), and 64% of consumers use Google Business Profile for contact information (LocalLeads247).</p>
<p>For contractors, the Map Pack — the three local results that appear above organic listings — is prime real estate. A contractor ranking in Position 1 of the Map Pack receives significantly more calls and website clicks than one ranking in Position 4 or below.</p>
<h3>Google Business Profile Optimization for Contractors</h3>
<p><strong>Claim and Verify</strong></p>
<p>The first step is non-negotiable: claim and verify your Google Business Profile. According to LocalLeads247, 56% of local businesses have not claimed their free listing. If you have not claimed yours, you are already behind half your competitors.</p>
<p><strong>Choose the Right Primary Category</strong></p>
<p>Your primary category should be as specific as possible. "General Contractor" is acceptable, but if you specialize, use the more specific category:<br />
- "General Contractor" (broad)<br />
- "Remodeling Contractor" (residential focus)<br />
- "Commercial Contractor" (B2B focus)</p>
<p>Add 5-10 secondary categories that cover your service mix.</p>
<p><strong>Service Area Configuration</strong></p>
<p>General contractors are typically service-area businesses without a customer-facing storefront. Hide your physical address and define service areas by city or ZIP code. Do not overreach — listing a 100-mile radius when you only work within 30 miles damages your relevance signals and generates unqualified leads.</p>
<p><strong>Photo Strategy</strong></p>
<p>Businesses with photos receive 42% more direction requests and 35% more website clicks (Google Support data via The Contractor Elite). Your photo strategy should include:<br />
- Before/after project photos (uploaded as sets)<br />
- Team photos (builds trust)<br />
- Equipment and vehicle photos (signals professionalism)<br />
- Office/shop photos (if applicable)<br />
- Logo and cover image</p>
<p>Upload 5-10 new photos monthly. Geo-tag photos when possible.</p>
<p><strong>Review Generation</strong></p>
<p>Reviews are the #1 ranking factor for Map Pack position. Google weighs three factors:<br />
- <strong>Volume</strong>: Total number of reviews<br />
- <strong>Velocity</strong>: How quickly you are getting new reviews<br />
- <strong>Recency</strong>: 25 recent reviews beats 45 old reviews</p>
<p>Best practices for contractors:<br />
- Send SMS review requests 24-48 hours after project completion (not email — SMS has higher open rates)<br />
- Include a direct review link (use Google's review link generator)<br />
- Respond to every review within 48 hours<br />
- Never offer incentives for reviews — Google can detect and remove them</p>
<p>A fully completed and optimized Google Business Profile can increase leads by 40%, and consistent reviews add another 25% (Changescape Web).</p>
<h3>Website Local SEO</h3>
<p><strong>NAP Consistency</strong></p>
<p>Your Name, Address, and Phone number must be identical across your website, Google Business Profile, Yelp, BBB, Facebook, and every directory listing. Even small variations ("St." vs. "Street," "Suite 100" vs. "#100") confuse search engines and dilute ranking signals.</p>
<p><strong>Location Pages</strong></p>
<p>If you serve multiple cities, create dedicated location pages for each primary service area. Each page should include:<br />
- Unique content (not duplicated across pages)<br />
- City-specific project examples<br />
- Local landmarks or neighborhood references<br />
- City-specific service descriptions<br />
- Embedded Google Map<br />
- Local schema markup</p>
<p>Example: /services/kitchen-remodeling-portland and /services/kitchen-remodeling-beaverton — each with unique project photos, local testimonials, and neighborhood-specific content.</p>
<p><strong>Local Business Schema</strong></p>
<p>Implement LocalBusiness schema markup on your contact page and homepage. This structured data helps search engines understand your service area, business hours, services offered, and contact information — increasing your chances of appearing in rich results and AI Overviews.</p>
<p><strong>Local Citations and Directories</strong></p>
<p>Beyond Google, ensure consistent listings on:<br />
- Yelp for Business<br />
- Better Business Bureau (BBB)<br />
- HomeAdvisor / Angi (controversial but still relevant for visibility)<br />
- Houzz<br />
- Thumbtack<br />
- Local chamber of commerce directories<br />
- Industry-specific directories (NARI, NAHB)</p>
<h3>Real-World Result</h3>
<p>Double G Consulting documented a contractor who started at Position 11 in the Map Pack (effectively invisible). After a 6-step GBP optimization process, they reached Position 3 at 60 days and Position 1 at 90 days. Inbound calls from Google more than tripled with zero additional ad spend.</p>
<p>[VISUAL: Map Pack ranking progression chart showing Position 11 → Position 3 → Position 1 over 90 days, with call volume increase]</p>
