<p>The key to success on these platforms is not volume — it is selectivity. According to Loopio's 2026 RFP data, 71% of high-win teams use formal go/no-go qualification frameworks. Chasing every RFP dilutes win rates and burns proposal resources.</p>
<h3>Property Manager Relationships</h3>
<p>Property managers are the gatekeepers to recurring commercial work. A single property management company may oversee dozens of properties, each requiring ongoing maintenance, tenant improvements, and capital projects.</p>
<p><strong>Building the Relationship</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify target property management firms</strong> in your service area using CoStar, LoopNet, or local commercial real estate databases</li>
<li><strong>Attend property management association events</strong> (IREM, BOMA local chapters)</li>
<li><strong>Offer preventive maintenance programs</strong> with scheduled inspections, priority response times, and discounted rates</li>
<li><strong>Provide documentation</strong> — work logs, photographic evidence, and detailed reporting that property managers need for their owners</li>
<li><strong>Propose a pilot project</strong> — a small job that demonstrates your reliability before larger commitments</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Maintenance Contract Structure</strong></p>
<p>Commercial maintenance contracts typically include:<br />
- Scope of work with specific, measurable tasks<br />
- Service Level Agreements (SLAs): 2-hour emergency response, 48-hour routine maintenance<br />
- Fixed monthly fees for predictable budgeting<br />
- 1-3 year terms with automatic renewal clauses<br />
- Insurance requirements: general liability ($1M-$3M), workers' compensation</p>
<h3>Go/No-Go Qualification Framework</h3>
<p>Before investing in any commercial proposal, score the opportunity on these criteria:</p>
