<h2>Chapter 7: Content and Nurture Marketing — Staying Top-of-Mind for 3-12 Months</h2>
<p>The general contractor's sales cycle is long. A homeowner who visits your website in January may not start their project until September. A commercial prospect who downloads your portfolio in Q1 may not release an RFP until Q4. If you are not nurturing these leads throughout the cycle, you are losing projects to competitors who stayed in touch.</p>
<h3>Why Nurture Marketing Is Essential for Contractors</h3>
<p>According to Venture Harbour's 2026 <a href="https://ventureharbour.com/20-insightful-lead-nurturing-statistics-charts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">lead nurturing statistics</a>, 48% of businesses say most of their leads require long-cycle nurturing. For construction specifically, sales cycles routinely stretch 6-12 months or longer (ZAG FIRST Marketing). Nurtured leads generate 50% more sales-ready opportunities at 33% lower cost than non-nurtured leads (Demand Gen Report).</p>
<p>The construction buyer's journey involves extensive research. 80% of Gen Z DIYers use YouTube for research, 61% of Gen X do the same, and 62% of Millennials do "a lot" or "a good amount" of research before hiring a contractor (Collective Measures 2026). Your content marketing job is to be present during that research phase — not just at the moment of decision.</p>
