<p>The contractors who win in 2026 are not the ones with the biggest budgets. They are the ones with the most systematic approach to attracting, nurturing, and converting high-ticket prospects. Start with the foundation. Build the system. Let it compound.</p>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ Section</h2>
<h3>How much should a general contractor spend on marketing?</h3>
<p>Most construction companies spend 1.8% to 3.2% of revenue on marketing (Siana Marketing 2026). Small businesses under 50 employees typically spend 3.2%, while larger firms spend 2.8-3.2%. Companies spending more than 5% on digital marketing report 28% higher lead conversion rates. For a contractor generating $2 million annually, a marketing budget of $60,000-$100,000 is appropriate.</p>
<h3>What is the average sales cycle for a general contractor?</h3>
<p>Residential remodeling sales cycles typically range from 3 to 9 months from initial inquiry to signed contract. Commercial construction sales cycles are longer, ranging from 6 to 18 months. According to Prospeo, 40.4% of B2B buyers take 6-12 months to make a purchasing decision. This long cycle makes nurture marketing essential — leads who are not nurtured will forget about you before they are ready to buy.</p>
<h3>How do I get more commercial construction leads?</h3>
<p>The most effective commercial lead generation strategies are: (1) LinkedIn Sales Navigator to identify facilities directors and property managers, (2) attending industry events (BOMA, IREM, local chamber), (3) building relationships with architects who influence contractor selection, (4) registering on commercial bidding platforms like Dodge Construction Network and ConstructConnect, and (5) creating content that demonstrates commercial expertise (case studies, whitepapers, LinkedIn articles). Cold bidding without relationships yields win rates of 15% or less.</p>
<h3>Should general contractors use HomeAdvisor or Angi?</h3>
<p>HomeAdvisor and Angi generate mixed results for general contractors. Some newer contractors find them useful for building an initial customer base and review portfolio. However, many experienced contractors report issues with shared leads, price competition, and low-quality inquiries. If you use these platforms, track cost per lead and close rate separately from other sources. Many successful contractors build their own lead generation through Google Business Profile, SEO, and referrals rather than relying on lead generation services.</p>
<h3>What is the best way to market a remodeling business?</h3>
<p>The most effective remodeling marketing combines: (1) Google Business Profile optimization for local Map Pack visibility, (2) a portfolio-driven website with case studies and before/after documentation, (3) Google Local Services Ads for high-intent leads, (4) review generation system targeting 5+ new Google reviews monthly, (5) email nurture sequences for prospects in the 3-9 month decision window, and (6) video content showing project transformations on YouTube, Instagram Reels, and TikTok.</p>
<h3>How important are online reviews for contractors?</h3>
<p>Online reviews are critical. 55% of homeowners read reviews before contacting a contractor (Collective Measures 2026). Google weighs review volume, velocity, and recency as primary Map Pack ranking factors. Contractors with 50+ Google reviews and a 4.8+ star average report significantly higher close rates than those with fewer reviews. The most effective review generation strategy is SMS requests sent 24-48 hours after project completion with a direct review link.</p>
<h3>What should a general contractor website include?</h3>
<p>A high-converting contractor website must include: (1) clear above-the-fold messaging with specific services and location, (2) tap-to-call phone number on mobile, (3) segmented portfolio with case studies, (4) dedicated service pages for each major offering, (5) trust signals (license, insurance, certifications, BBB), (6) simple contact form (3 fields max for initial inquiry), (7) financing information for projects over $25,000, (8) client testimonials with full names and project details, and (9) fast mobile load time (under 2.5 seconds).</p>
<h3>How do I build relationships with architects?</h3>
<p>Start by attending AIA chapter events consistently without pitching. Offer value first — share relevant project photos, industry insights, or co-authored content. Deliver flawlessly on the first project together, as it serves as an audition for future referrals. Respect design intent, communicate proactively, and finish on time and on budget. After establishing trust, explore formal co-marketing like joint portfolio pages, shared case studies, and referral agreements.</p>
<h3>What is a good conversion rate for a contractor website?</h3>
<p>The average contractor website converts at 2-4%. Good performance is 5-8%, and elite performance is 8-15% (PipelineOn 2026). Remodeling and construction sites typically convert at 3-7% due to longer consideration cycles. The fastest way to improve conversion is adding a sticky mobile tap-to-call button (+25-40% call volume), reducing mobile load time under 2.5 seconds, and simplifying contact forms to 3 fields or fewer.</p>
<h3>How long does SEO take to work for contractors?</h3>
<p>SEO for contractors typically shows initial results in 3-6 months and significant results in 6-12 months. Google Business Profile optimization can produce visible Map Pack movement in 30-90 days. Website SEO for competitive keywords requires consistent content publishing, citation building, and backlink acquisition. SEO delivers the highest long-term ROI of any marketing channel — 400-450% according to Siana Marketing — but requires patience and consistent investment.</p>
<h3>What is the best paid advertising channel for general contractors?</h3>
<p>Google Local Services Ads are the highest-ROI paid channel for most residential contractors due to pay-per-lead pricing ($20-$50/lead) and top-of-page placement. Google Search campaigns work well for contractors with 30+ monthly conversions to feed Smart Bidding algorithms. LinkedIn Ads are essential for commercial contractors targeting facilities directors and property managers. Facebook/Instagram ads work best for visual awareness campaigns and retargeting.</p>
<h3>How do I track marketing ROI for my contracting business?</h3>
<p>Calculate marketing ROI as: (Revenue from marketing - Marketing cost) / Marketing cost. To track accurately: (1) use unique phone numbers per channel via call tracking, (2) add UTM parameters to all campaign URLs, (3) require "How did you hear about us?" on every intake form, (4) tag every lead with source in your CRM, (5) track proposals and wins by source monthly, and (6) calculate average project value by lead source to identify your most profitable channels.</p>
<h3>What is the difference between marketing for residential and commercial contracting?</h3>
<p>Residential marketing targets homeowners with 3-9 month sales cycles, emotional decision-making, and search behavior on Google and social media. Commercial marketing targets facilities directors and property managers with 6-18 month sales cycles, committee-based decisions, and professional networking on LinkedIn and at industry events. Residential marketing emphasizes portfolio visuals, reviews, and financing. Commercial marketing emphasizes safety records, bonding capacity, schedule adherence, and relationship-building.</p>
<h3>Should I niche down as a general contractor?</h3>
<p>Niching down makes marketing significantly easier. A contractor who specializes in "historic home renovations in Portland" can dominate that niche's search results, command premium pricing, and attract better-fit clients than a generalist. However, niching requires sufficient market size to support your revenue goals. Many successful contractors use a "primary niche + secondary services" model — leading with a specialty while accepting broader work.</p>
<h3>How do I handle seasonality in contractor marketing?</h3>
<p>Construction marketing has natural seasonality, with peak demand in spring and fall. Counter-seasonal strategies include: (1) running "beat the rush" campaigns in winter for spring scheduling, (2) promoting indoor projects (basements, interiors) during slow seasons, (3) building maintenance contract revenue for year-round cash flow, (4) increasing ad spend 30-50% 60 days before peak season, and (5) using slow periods for portfolio documentation, content creation, and website improvements.</p>
